=> Easiest way to install WSL

Microsoft currently recommends using the wsl --install command. It enables the required components, installs WSL 2, and installs Ubuntu by default.

Step 1 — Open PowerShell as Administrator

In Windows:

Start
  ↓
Search "PowerShell"
  ↓
Right-click PowerShell
  ↓
Run as administrator

You'll get something like:

Administrator: Windows PowerShell

Step 2 — Run this command
wsl --install

Press Enter.

Windows will install the required components and Ubuntu.

Microsoft says this command requires Windows 10 version 2004 (Build 19041) or later, or Windows 11.

Step 3 — Restart your computer

After installation, Windows may ask you to restart.

Restart the computer.

=> After restarting

Ubuntu should start automatically, or you can open it from:

Start
   ↓
Ubuntu

The first time you start Ubuntu, it will take some time to initialize.

Then you'll be asked to create:

Enter new UNIX username:

For example:

bilal

Then:

Enter new UNIX password:

Important: When you type the Linux password, you won't see characters appearing on the screen. That's normal.

Type the password and press Enter.

=> Now you have Linux

You'll eventually see something similar to:

bilal@DESKTOP-XXXX:~$

This means you're inside Ubuntu.

Try:

pwd

Then:

ls

Then:

whoami

You can also check the Linux version:

cat /etc/os-release

=> Check whether you're using WSL 2

Open PowerShell and run:

wsl -l -v

You should see something similar to:

  NAME      STATE           VERSION
* Ubuntu    Running         2

The important part is:

VERSION
2

That means you're running WSL 2.

New installations through wsl --install use WSL 2 by default.

=> What should you install after Ubuntu?

Since you're using it for your Linux + Ansible + DevOps practice, I would do this:

First update Ubuntu

Inside Ubuntu:

sudo apt update

Then:

sudo apt upgrade -y

Then check:

python3 --version

git --version

ssh -V
